// fx_atan2 -- the inverse tangent, in millidegrees.
//
// New here: nothing in id_development or idem has one, and every aiming, orbit,
// bearing and polar conversion needs it. The result is in (-180000, 180000], the
// same convention as C's atan2, with the sign of y choosing the half-plane so
// that a full turn is representable and (0, 0) answers 0 rather than aborting.
//
// **It is a binary search over fx_sin/fx_cos, not a table of its own.** The
// point (ax, ay) lies at or above the ray at angle m exactly when
// ay*cos(m) >= ax*sin(m), and that test is monotone in m -- so seventeen halvings
// of [0, 45001) land on the answer to the millidegree, using the 91 numbers the
// library already has. A second table would be 91 more constants to maintain, a
// second quadrant fold to get wrong, and no more accurate: the limit here is the
// x1000 scale of the sine, not the search.
//
// **Accuracy: within 81 millidegrees (0.081 degrees) of the true angle**, worst
// case, measured over the 2601 points of a 51x51 integer grid plus 6000 random
// pairs up to 10^7 (python3 model against math.atan2). The error comes entirely
// from the sine being quantised to a thousandth: near an axis a thousandth of
// unit length subtends about 57 millidegrees. Both axes and both diagonals are
// *exact*, which is what idstd_fx_atan_oct's octant fold buys.
//
// Cost is about 17 iterations, each one an fx_sin and an fx_cos, so roughly 34
// interpolated table reads per call. That is fine per entity per frame and wrong
// per pixel.

fx_atan2(int idstd_y, int idstd_x) {
  int idstd_q = idstd_fx_atan_absq(idstd_y, idstd_x);
  int idstd_ret_i = idstd_fx_atan_fold(idstd_y, idstd_x, idstd_q);
} return int idstd_ret_i;
(0, 5):(0)
(5, 0):(90000)
(0, -5):(180000)
(-5, 0):(-90000)

// Both operands into the first quadrant, then the angle there -- the step
// fx_atan2 starts from, split out so its own block stays inside the limit.
idstd_fx_atan_absq(int idstd_y, int idstd_x) {
  int idstd_fx_abs_v = fx_abs(idstd_x);
  int idstd_fx_abs_v2 = fx_abs(idstd_y);
  int idstd_ret_i = idstd_fx_atan_q(idstd_fx_abs_v, idstd_fx_abs_v2);
} return int idstd_ret_i;
(0, 5):(0)
(5, 0):(90000)

// The first-quadrant angle q placed in the right half-plane. x < 0 reflects it
// about the vertical axis; y < 0 then negates the whole thing, which is why the
// two tests are separate statements rather than a four-way branch that would not
// fit in one block.
idstd_fx_atan_fold(int idstd_y, int idstd_x, int idstd_q) {
  int idstd_deg = idstd_q;
  if (idstd_x < 0) {
    idstd_deg = 180000 - idstd_q;
  }
  if (idstd_y < 0) {
    idstd_deg = 0 - idstd_deg;
  }
} return int idstd_deg;
(10, 10, 30000):(30000)
(10, -10, 30000):(150000)
(-10, -10, 30000):(-150000)
